WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.109 --> 00:00:02.170
(gentle music)

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:05.180
- [Narrator] Coral reefs,
their bright, vivid colors

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.400
can be seen in tropical ocean
waters around the globe.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.150
Beyond their brilliant appearance

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.223
lies a hidden significance.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.183
Coral are animals.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.880
Though they may look like colorful plants,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.403
coral are, in fact, made up
of tiny animals called polyps.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.860
These invertebrates can range
from the size of a pinhead

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.823
to a bit larger than a basketball.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:40.030
Each polyp consists of
a soft, saclike body

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.580
topped by a mouth covered
in stinging tentacles.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.680
To protect their soft
bodies and add support,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.193
the polyps secrete limestone
skeletons, or calicles.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.353
Corals are mega builders.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.330
Polyp calicles connect to one another,

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:03.450
creating a colony that
acts as a single organism.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.670
As colonies grow over hundreds
and thousands of years,

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.430
they join with other colonies

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.970
and become reefs that can grow
to hundreds of miles long.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:18.070
The largest coral reef is
Australia's Great Barrier Reef,

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.763
which began growing
about 20,000 years ago.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.970
Coral reefs are some of the most

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.273
diverse ecosystems on Earth.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.853
Though they cover less
than 1% of the ocean floor,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:40.063
coral reefs are home to 25%
of all marine creatures.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.620
It's been estimated that
up to two million species

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.140
inhabit coral reefs,
rivaling the biodiversity

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.180
of the rain forest.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.350
The reefs provide rich habitat

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.863
that helps protect
young fish as they grow.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.813
Coral are translucent.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.160
Coral reefs get their
rainbow of colors from algae,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.113
or zooxanthellae, that
live in their tissue.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.400
Though corals use their
tentacles to capture some food,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.353
most of their food comes
from the algae they house.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.030
When coral become stressed by
pollution or other factors,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.433
they evict their algae.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.903
Coral bleaching results,
revealing corals' white skeletons.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.463
Coral provide a window to the past.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.550
As coral grow, their limestone
skeletons form layers,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.860
similar to tree rings,
that vary in composition

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.780
and thickness based on ocean
conditions at the time.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.700
With some coral reefs growing

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.720
for thousands or even millions of years,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.760
scientists can study these layers

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.650
to reveal what the Earth's climate

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.900
may have been like in the ancient past.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.620
Unfortunately, climate change is putting

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.760
coral's future in danger,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.260
along with the millions of
species that inhabit the reefs

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.920
and the half-billion people
that rely on reef fish for food.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.020
Warming waters result in prolonged

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.840
coral bleaching that kill coral reefs

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.223
or leave them vulnerable to other threats.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.040
Without significant
action on climate change,

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.220
our oceans could lose many
of their colorful reefs

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.933
by the end of the century.

